# The unparser's round-trip tests 2, 3 and 4 (design document, "Unparser
# and round-trip tests") on the Mercury files in the tests that convert
# without errors: unparsing exactly gives back the file and reports nothing,
# the canonical layout is its own canonical layout, and the canonical and
# parenthesized forms have the file's parse tree. Files with errors are
# checked by the fuzz target instead (../fuzz_corpus.test).
#
# RUN: %round-trip %S/../convert/clauses.m %S/../convert/declarations.m \
# RUN:   %S/../convert/definitions.m %S/../convert/directives.m \
# RUN:   %S/../convert/pragmas.m %S/../convert/typeclasses.m \
# RUN:   %S/../../Lexer/tokens.m %S/../../Lexer/directives.m \
# RUN:   %S/../../Lexer/line_directives.m %S/../../Driver/Inputs/sumsq.m \
# RUN:   %S/../Inputs/fuzz/byte_order_mark.m %S/exact.m \
# RUN:   %S/Inputs/messy.m %S/Inputs/messy.canonical.m \
# RUN:   %S/../convert/omp.m %S/../../../utils/term-oracle/term_oracle.m
#
# With -fopenmp, on the files with %$omp directives, which are then part of
# the parse tree:
# RUN: %round-trip --openmp %S/../convert/omp.m %S/../../Lexer/directives.m \
# RUN:   %S/../../Driver/Inputs/sumsq.m
#
# With -fdo-loops too, on the files with loops:
# RUN: %round-trip --openmp --do-loops %S/../convert/loops.m \
# RUN:   %S/Inputs/loops-messy.m %S/Inputs/loops.canonical.m
